Determine whether the supplied memory evidence supports an exact answer to the request.

Memory records are concise representations derived from claims. Their structured timing is a normalized form of
source-relative time. Source evidence contains the cited conversational turn and a small amount of adjacent dialogue;
lines marked `cited` are direct claim evidence, while lines marked `context` help resolve references and surrounding
meaning. Use the source conversation time and structured timing for temporal questions.

Identify the requested subject, relation, object, and time constraint, then verify them together from the supplied
evidence. Return the shortest exact answer span. For a request asking for a date, return only the resolved date. For
yes/no, return only Yes or No. Set `answerable` to false when the supplied evidence does not establish the requested
relation.
